From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:28:46 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] usb: roles: Let a switch be held in a role while requests are
 recorded

A component that sits between a dual-role controller and its connector
can have to take the controller out of its role for a moment, without
having a say in what the role is. The Qualcomm USB4 host router is one:
the SuperSpeed port of the controller it tunnels is routed either to the
router or to the PHY, and the route must not change under a running
host. The role itself is decided by the Type-C driver, from what the
partner is.

Setting the role and setting it back with usb_role_switch_set_role()
does not do: what to set it back to is a guess. The Type-C driver can
ask for another role in between, because the partner was unplugged or
the data role was swapped, and the role that is written back then
overrules it. Nothing tells it to ask again.

Add usb_role_switch_hold(), which sets a role and makes
usb_role_switch_set_role() record what it is asked for instead of
applying it, and usb_role_switch_unhold(), which ends that and sets the
role that was asked for last. A switch that is not held behaves as
before.

The holder has to know that the role it asked for is in effect, and a
switch may change its role after its set callback has returned. Such a
switch can provide a sync callback, which returns once the role given
to set last is in effect; the hold calls it. usb_role_switch_get_role()
cannot stand in for it: a switch that has not started on a role it was
asked for a moment ago still reports the one before.

A switch that is in the role already is left as it is, and the release
does not set a role again that the switch was given last. Holding a
switch that needs no change makes no call to its set callback.

For a switch without a get callback the role that was set last with
success is kept apart from the one that was asked for last, so that the
role reported during a hold, and after a release that failed, is the one
the switch is in.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/usb/roles/class.c | 134 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 include/linux/usb/role.h  |  19 ++++++
 2 files changed, 145 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/roles/class.c b/drivers/usb/roles/class.c
index edec139b6..9b26036e4 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/roles/class.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/roles/class.c
@@ -25,7 +25,9 @@ struct usb_role_switch {
 	struct lock_class_key key;
 	struct mutex lock; /* device lock*/
 	struct module *module; /* the module this device depends on */
-	enum usb_role role;
+	enum usb_role role; /* asked for last */
+	enum usb_role applied; /* given to @set last, with success */
+	bool held; /* role requests are recorded, not applied */
 	bool registered;
 
 	/* From descriptor */
@@ -34,6 +36,7 @@ struct usb_role_switch {
 	struct device *udc;
 	usb_role_switch_set_t set;
 	usb_role_switch_get_t get;
+	usb_role_switch_sync_t sync;
 	bool allow_userspace_control;
 };
 
@@ -84,10 +87,17 @@ int usb_role_switch_set_role(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
 
 	mutex_lock(&sw->lock);
 
-	ret = sw->set(sw, role);
-	if (!ret) {
+	if (sw->held) {
+		/* Applied by usb_role_switch_unhold() */
 		sw->role = role;
-		kobject_uevent(&sw->dev.kobj, KOBJ_CHANGE);
+		ret = 0;
+	} else {
+		ret = sw->set(sw, role);
+		if (!ret) {
+			sw->role = role;
+			sw->applied = role;
+			kobject_uevent(&sw->dev.kobj, KOBJ_CHANGE);
+		}
 	}
 
 	mutex_unlock(&sw->lock);
@@ -96,6 +106,116 @@ int usb_role_switch_set_role(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(usb_role_switch_set_role);
 
+/* The role the switch is in, as far as it can tell. Called with the lock held. */
+static enum usb_role usb_role_switch_current(struct usb_role_switch *sw)
+{
+	return sw->get ? sw->get(sw) : sw->applied;
+}
+
+/**
+ * usb_role_switch_hold - Put a switch into a role and keep it there
+ * @sw: USB role switch
+ * @role: USB role to hold the switch in
+ *
+ * For a component that has to take the controller behind @sw out of its role
+ * for a while without deciding the role itself, for example to change what
+ * the controller's port is connected to. Until usb_role_switch_unhold(), the
+ * role usb_role_switch_set_role() is asked for is recorded instead of applied,
+ * and that call returns 0, also when the role cannot be set at the release:
+ * whoever decides the role is not overruled, only made to wait.
+ *
+ * @role is in effect when this returns. A switch that changes its role after
+ * its set callback has returned provides the sync callback for that; without
+ * one, the role is taken to be in effect when the set callback returns. A
+ * switch that is in @role already is left as it is.
+ *
+ * This can sleep for as long as the switch takes to change its role, the
+ * removal of a host for example, with the lock of @sw held, under which the
+ * sync callback is called. It must not be called while tasks are frozen for
+ * system sleep: a switch may change its role from a freezable workqueue.
+ *
+ * The caller keeps its reference to @sw until usb_role_switch_unhold(). There
+ * is one holder at a time, and usb_role_switch_unhold() is for the holder
+ * only: it does not check who calls.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 on success, -EBUSY if @sw is held already, -ENODEV if @sw is not
+ * registered, or the error of the switch. @sw is held only on success.
+ */
+int usb_role_switch_hold(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(sw) || !sw->registered)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	mutex_lock(&sw->lock);
+
+	if (sw->held) {
+		ret = -EBUSY;
+	} else {
+		/* A role asked for a moment ago may not be in effect yet */
+		if (sw->sync)
+			sw->sync(sw);
+		ret = 0;
+		if (usb_role_switch_current(sw) != role) {
+			ret = sw->set(sw, role);
+			if (!ret) {
+				sw->applied = role;
+				if (sw->sync)
+					sw->sync(sw);
+				kobject_uevent(&sw->dev.kobj, KOBJ_CHANGE);
+			}
+		}
+		if (!ret)
+			sw->held = true;
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&sw->lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(usb_role_switch_hold);
+
+/**
+ * usb_role_switch_unhold - Let a held switch take the role it was asked for
+ * @sw: USB role switch
+ *
+ * Ends usb_role_switch_hold() and sets the role that was asked for last with
+ * usb_role_switch_set_role(), before or during the hold, unless that is the
+ * role the switch was given last. It does not wait for the role to be in
+ * effect.
+ *
+ * Returns 0 on success, -ENODEV if @sw is not registered any more (the hold
+ * ends with the switch then), or the error of the switch, in which case @sw
+ * is no longer held, stays in the role it was held in and, if it has no get
+ * callback, reports that role.
+ */
+int usb_role_switch_unhold(struct usb_role_switch *sw)
+{
+	int ret = 0;
+
+	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(sw) || !sw->registered)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	mutex_lock(&sw->lock);
+
+	if (sw->held) {
+		sw->held = false;
+		if (sw->role != sw->applied) {
+			ret = sw->set(sw, sw->role);
+			if (!ret) {
+				sw->applied = sw->role;
+				kobject_uevent(&sw->dev.kobj, KOBJ_CHANGE);
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&sw->lock);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(usb_role_switch_unhold);
+
 /**
  * usb_role_switch_get_role - Get the USB role for a switch
  * @sw: USB role switch
@@ -112,10 +232,7 @@ enum usb_role usb_role_switch_get_role(struct usb_role_switch *sw)
 
 	mutex_lock(&sw->lock);
 
-	if (sw->get)
-		role = sw->get(sw);
-	else
-		role = sw->role;
+	role = usb_role_switch_current(sw);
 
 	mutex_unlock(&sw->lock);
 
@@ -382,6 +499,7 @@ usb_role_switch_register(struct device *parent,
 	sw->udc = desc->udc;
 	sw->set = desc->set;
 	sw->get = desc->get;
+	sw->sync = desc->sync;
 
 	sw->module = parent->driver->owner;
 	sw->dev.parent = parent;
diff --git a/include/linux/usb/role.h b/include/linux/usb/role.h
index b5deafd91..a5d21df6f 100644
--- a/include/linux/usb/role.h
+++ b/include/linux/usb/role.h
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@ enum usb_role {
 typedef int (*usb_role_switch_set_t)(struct usb_role_switch *sw,
 				     enum usb_role role);
 typedef enum usb_role (*usb_role_switch_get_t)(struct usb_role_switch *sw);
+typedef void (*usb_role_switch_sync_t)(struct usb_role_switch *sw);
 
 /**
  * struct usb_role_switch_desc - USB Role Switch Descriptor
@@ -25,6 +26,10 @@ typedef enum usb_role (*usb_role_switch_get_t)(struct usb_role_switch *sw);
  * @udc: Optional reference to the peripheral controller device
  * @set: Callback for setting the role
  * @get: Callback for getting the role (optional)
+ * @sync: Callback that returns once the role given to @set last is in effect
+ *	(optional; for a switch that changes its role after @set has returned).
+ *	Called with the lock of the switch held; it may sleep and must not call
+ *	usb_role_switch_set_role() and the like for the same switch.
  * @allow_userspace_control: If true userspace may change the role through sysfs
  * @driver_data: Private data pointer
  * @name: Name for the switch (optional)
@@ -42,6 +47,7 @@ struct usb_role_switch_desc {
 	struct device *udc;
 	usb_role_switch_set_t set;
 	usb_role_switch_get_t get;
+	usb_role_switch_sync_t sync;
 	bool allow_userspace_control;
 	void *driver_data;
 	const char *name;
@@ -51,6 +57,8 @@ struct usb_role_switch_desc {
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_USB_ROLE_SWITCH)
 int usb_role_switch_set_role(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role);
 enum usb_role usb_role_switch_get_role(struct usb_role_switch *sw);
+int usb_role_switch_hold(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role);
+int usb_role_switch_unhold(struct usb_role_switch *sw);
 struct usb_role_switch *usb_role_switch_get(struct device *dev);
 struct usb_role_switch *fwnode_usb_role_switch_get(struct fwnode_handle *node);
 void usb_role_switch_put(struct usb_role_switch *sw);
@@ -78,6 +86,17 @@ static inline enum usb_role usb_role_switch_get_role(struct usb_role_switch *sw)
 	return USB_ROLE_NONE;
 }
 
+static inline int usb_role_switch_hold(struct usb_role_switch *sw,
+		enum usb_role role)
+{
+	return -ENODEV;
+}
+
+static inline int usb_role_switch_unhold(struct usb_role_switch *sw)
+{
+	return -ENODEV;
+}
+
 static inline struct usb_role_switch *usb_role_switch_get(struct device *dev)
 {
 	return ERR_PTR(-ENODEV);
-- 
2.56.1

